Resonance

License: GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 or later (/LICENSE)

Resonance is a high-fidelity simulator and regression harness for
Concord (https://github.com/podomy/concord): it runs real Concord
binaries in isolated network namespaces over a simulated radio mesh,
and asserts cluster convergence across partition, fault, and scale
scenarios. It was primarily made for Concord to create a flywheel of
software and simulation.

Build and test

  make                    # build ./bin/resonance
  make concord            # fetch Concord from GitHub and rebuild if new
  make check              # fast checks, no Concord
  sudo make check-full    # all check/ scenarios, needs ./bin/concord
  make suite-lint         # validate scenarios/*.yaml + suite/examples/*.yaml, no privileges
  make log                # run demo, tee output to current.log
  make clean              # remove binaries and test artifacts

YAML scenarios run through bin/rsuite (built with make bin/rsuite
via the bin/rsuite target):

  bin/rsuite lint <file.yaml>...    # validate without running
  bin/rsuite run <file.yaml>...     # run (needs root for netns + TUN)
  bin/rsuite list <file.yaml>...    # parse-only summary, no privileges
  bin/rsuite doctor                 # report on setup and dependencies

Every make target above exists in the Makefile: all (the bare make),
concord, check, check-full, suite-lint, log, clean, and the file
target bin/rsuite. Note that suite-lint is missing from the .PHONY
list (which names only all, check, check-full, clean, concord, log),
so a file named suite-lint in the worktree shadows the lint target.

Scenario files live in scenarios/*.yaml (curated set) and
suite/examples/*.yaml (minimal ports of check/ shapes).

Documentation

  Start at /docs/overview.md. It explains the six layers every
  scenario is built from, one file per layer (/docs/layer0-sim.md,
  /docs/layer1-mesh.md, /docs/layer2-lifecycle.md,
  /docs/layer3-faults.md, /docs/layer4-observation.md,
  /docs/layer5-runner.md), plus a separate note on test speed
  (/docs/speed.md) and a glossary of terms (/docs/glossary.md).

Contributing

Discuss your change with the engineering team at contact@podomy.com
before opening a PR in order not to waste anybody's effort or time.

Announcements and engineering updates on distributed systems, consensus
algorithms, and robotic fleet coordination are shared via our newsletter
at https://podomy.com.

  Commit message format - /COMMITS
  Contributor license agreement - /CLA
  Contributing - /CONTRIBUTING

